From f2676883e27fc0a2693f08e23026165ccbaf16d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The browser Authors <noreply@browser.invalid>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:18:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fontations-natural-mode

DirectWrite's natural mode in Skia's Fontations port, for a Linux build
presenting Windows (the linux-fonts commit switches it on there). Chrome on
Windows draws small text through DirectWrite, which picks its natural mode
for a face and size when the font's gasp range lacks symmetric smoothing,
or, without a gasp range, up to 20 px on a face with instructions: the
font's instructions run with the symmetric-rendering bit off and the outline
left alone across the line, and the glyph is antialiased across the line
only, as ClearType's three subpixels of six samples each averaged to grey,
so a pixel takes one of 19 levels before gamma. Linux Chrome draws the same
fonts with Fontations, antialiased in both directions. With
SkTypeface_Fontations_SetWindowsNaturalMode(true), Fontations' scaler
context makes the same choice from the gasp table, runs the instructions as
natural mode does (skrifa's smooth target, symmetric rendering off), and
renders an A8 glyph from 18 aliased samples across each pixel, then the
preblend gamma, as the DirectWrite port applies it. Off by default; nothing
changes unless a caller turns it on.
